<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bochnia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bochnia is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, known for its salt mine, one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where rock salt was mined from the mid-13th century until 1990. Together with the mine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wieliczka">Wieliczka</a> it formed the royal salt works of Kraków, a major source of crown revenue, and it is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bochnia.jpg" alt="Bochnia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Januszk57 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bochnia._Rynek_20._Budynek_dawnego_klasztoru_Dominikan%C3%B3w_A_2_M_n1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bochnia is one of the oldest towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, on the river Raba. The first known source is a letter of 1198 in which Aymar the Monk, Latin Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, confirmed a donation by the local magnate Mikora Gryfit to the monastery of the Order of the Holy Sepulchre in Miechów. The discovery of rock salt at the site of the present mine in 1248 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Bolesław V the Chaste</a> to grant city privileges under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> law on 27 February 1253. Many residents were German-speaking settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>. Under Casimir the Great in the 14th century a town hall, a defensive wall and a hospital for miners were built, and work began on St Nicholas Basilica.</p><p>A fire in 1561 burned the town down, and its salt deposits were depleted, which began a decline. Bochnia was captured by the Swedes in 1655, the Transylvanians in 1657 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> in 1662, and by the 1660s only 54 houses stood. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> destroyed it in 1702. In 1772 it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy in the First Partition of Poland and remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> until 1918. The Austrians tore down the town hall and wall, but the creation of Bochnia County in 1867 and the Galician Railway began a slow recovery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Germans shot 52 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in Bochnia in 1939 in one of the first mass executions in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. About 3,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in the town at the outbreak of the war, around a fifth of the population, and the Germans made it the site of a ghetto. The Jewish community was murdered in the Holocaust apart from about 200 forced labourers, and about 90 Jews from Bochnia survived the war.</p><p>The Bochnia Salt Mine, established between the 12th and 13th centuries, is regarded as the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. It is 4.5 kilometres long and 468 metres deep, with 16 levels, and its deserted chambers form an underground town open to visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bochnia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mongols devastated the duchy in 1241, but under Boleslaw V the Chaste Krakow received town rights in 1257 and the salt mines of Wieliczka and <b>Bochnia</b> brought wealth.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li></ul></section>
